  "Bug_Title": "Classes that don't define \"hashCode()\" should not be used in hashes",
  "Bug_Details": "Because Object implements hashCode , any Java class can be put into a hash structure. However, classes that define equals(Object) but not hashCode() aren\u2019t truly hash-able because instances that are equivalent according to the equals method can return different hashes.\nNoncompliant code example\npublic class Student {  // no hashCode() method; not hash-able\n  // ...\n\n  public boolean equals(Object o) {\n    // ...\n  }\n}\n\npublic class School {\n  private Map<Student, Integer> studentBody = // okay so far\n          new HashTable<Student, Integer>(); // Noncompliant\n\n  // ...\nCompliant solution\npublic class Student {  // has hashCode() method; hash-able\n  // ...\n\n  public boolean equals(Object o) {\n    // ...\n  }\n  public int hashCode() {\n    // ...\n  }\n}\n\npublic class School {\n  private Map<Student, Integer> studentBody = new HashTable<Student, Integer>();\n\n  // ...",
  "Bug_Explanation": "The Student class doesn't override hashCode method which is necessary for the objects of this class to be used in hash-based collections like HashMap. This leads to duplicate entries in the map.",
  "Buggy_Line": [
    13
  ],
  "Seed_Compilation_Status": "Successful",
  "Trigger_Explanation": "The bug is triggered because the equals method is implemented in the Student class but the hashCode method is not. The equals method compares the name attribute of the Student objects, but the hashCode method is not implemented. Without the hashCode method, the Student objects can't be used as keys in a HashMap. So when we try to add the same Student object twice, it is treated as two different objects and can be added to the map causing the size to increase.",
  "Trigger_Line": [
